 Neoplasm", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.043, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Subacute presentation moderately supports CML (B2) compared to acute processes like lymphoid neoplasms (B3) or plasma cell disorders (B4)."]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Plasma Cell Disorder", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.062, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Subacute presentation moderately supports CML (B2) compared to acute processes like lymphoid neoplasms (B3) or plasma cell disorders (B4)."]}}, "frontier": ["B1", "B2"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the normal differential and lack of lymphocytosis rule out lymphoid neoplasm as the underlying cause?", "summary": "Normal differential and no lymphocytosis moderately exclude lymphoid neoplasm (B3) as the primary mechanism."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of decreased circulating antibody level (not mentioned in findings) argue against plasma cell disorder in favor of myeloid neoplasm?", "summary": "Lack of antibody suppression provides weak evidence against plasma cell disorder (B4), but myeloid neoplasm (B2) remains plausible."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the elevated leukocyte alkaline phosphatase (LAP) strongly support reactive leukocytosis (B1) over myeloid neoplasm (B2), given CML's typical low LAP profile?", "summary": "Elevated LAP strongly supports reactive leukocytosis (B1) and decisively excludes myeloid neoplasm (B2) like CML."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the subacute onset (3 weeks) with constitutional symptoms more strongly suggest chronic myeloid leukemia (CML) than acute lymphoblastic lymphoma (B3) or multiple myeloma (B4)?", "summary": "Subacute presentation moderately supports CML (B2) compared to acute processes like lym