rders (B4).", "Weight loss and hypercalcemia moderately support neoplastic causes (B2) but do not eliminate B1 as a possible explanation.", "Hypercalcemia and constitutional symptoms moderately favor neoplastic causes (B2) over reactive leukocytosis (B1)."], "evidence_against": ["Elevated LAP with normal differential supports reactive leukocytosis (B1) over myeloid neoplasms (B2), consistent with leukemoid reaction patterns.", "Hypercalcemia provides weak support for plasma cell disorder (B4) but is insufficient to strongly favor it over myeloid neoplasms (B2).", "Elevated LAP strongly argues against myeloid neoplasm (B2) and favors leukemoid reaction (B1), consistent with CML's typical low LAP profile.", "Elevated LAP strongly supports reactive leukocytosis (B1) and decisively excludes myeloid neoplasm (B2) like CML."], "unresolved_questions": [], "askable_discriminators": ["History of splenomegaly or prior MPN diagnosis", "Presence of blasts on peripheral smear"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Bone marrow biopsy", "Cytogenetics (e.g., BCR-ABL1, JAK2, CALR, MPL)", "Flow cytometry for blast lineage"]}, "target_level": 2}
